The NetHack spell system has used a power stat ever since it was
introduced by Mike Stephenson in the first NetHack to be Net. A brief
(and hopefully accurate; correct me if I've got anything wrong)
overview of the history may be useful...

Originally, spellbooks were used up when you read them (or rather,
transcribed them into your own spellbook), but you never forgot the
spells. Casting was fairly straightforward: there were no class-based
modifiers, metal penalty, or intelligence/wisdom requirement (the last
for the excellent reason that stats other than strength hadn't yet
been introduced), the failure rate being a dieroll against a
combination of your XL, Luck, and the spell's level.

Forgetting spells was introduced in 2.2a (though the books still got
used up when read, making life no fun for a spellcaster), along with
the Amulet's additional mana drain; spells had a random number of uses
between 2 and 10-spelllevel.

3.0 made a number of changes to the spell system, as with so much else
about the game. Learning spells was moved from the X ('transcribe')
command to r)ead. Intelligence (this being the version that added the
six-stat system) was added to the formula for uncursed spellbooks
having cursed effects, and to the chance of casting failure (where it
replaced XL).

3.1 stopped spellbooks being used up when read, and to compensate
reduced their number of uses to between 1 and 9-spelllevel; each
spellbook now had between 1 and 8-spelllevel reads in it. Confusion on
reading a forgotten spell first appeared in this version.

3.2 made significant changes again. The number of uses of a spell were
increased to a huge 30 - spelllevel, and the chance of a book being
too faint to read made a random (but small) chance modified by the
number of reads. Failure rate calculations were changed to pretty much
the complex beastie we know today: different roles were given
different spellcasting modifiers, their own specialist spell, and
split between INT-users and WIS-users; metallic armour and shields
were given penalties, and XL was added back into the equation.

3.3 implemented the 'wizard patch', as it was known. Time rather than
use became the criterion for forgetting a spell. Spells were
integrated into the skills system that had been added for weapons in
3.2, each spell being allocated to a school and different roles being
allowed to enhance their skill in certain schools to improve their
casting chance and, in some cases, the spells' effects. Intelligent
Wizards' hunger penalty reduction was also added here, along with
their ability to sense if a spellbook might be too hard to read. Robes
were added to improve casting chances.

Since then, there have been a few further tweaks; 3.4.0 let lenses
improve spell-learning ability and speed, while 3.4.1 finally got the
behaviour of reading spellbooks when confused working as it was
intended to have been, and added stunning as a possibility when
casting a forgotten spell.


Which of these changes were you thinking of, Boudewijn?

